## Write your own tools

A tool is an async function marked `@server.tool(name="{{ server_id }}.<tool>")` in
`src/{{ package }}/server.py`. It does not need a data source. After you add, change or
remove tools:

1. Write a test that calls the tool as a role (copy one in `tests/`). If an agent is linked to
   this server, the test `tests/test_<agent>_with_{{ package }}.py` in the workspace's own
   `tests/` folder calls one of this server's tools: update it too.
2. From the workspace root, run `"%PYTHON%" -m agentcraft2_cli registry pin {{ server_id }}`.
   It adds new tools to `registry/mcp-tools.yaml`, drops tools you removed and pins every
   input schema. A new tool starts as confidential and not read-only: open its entry and set
   `classification`, `read_only` and `description`.
3. Run `"%PYTHON%" -m agentcraft2_cli check`.

{% if sample %}
**Replacing the sample.** `greet`, `people_by_team`, `data/`, `queries/` and the
`{{ var.data_sources }}` line in `.env.example` and `.env` belong together. Once your own
tools replace them, remove all of them, and remove `source = services.data_source(DATA_SOURCE)`
and `DATA_SOURCE` from `server.py` and `__init__.py`. The rules `...-managers-see-everything`
and `...-analysts-see-no-email` in the workspace's `policies/agentcraft2/rules/data.yaml` and
their samples then guard nothing and can go too.

{% endif %}
**Telling the model why a call failed.** Raise
`ToolError("reason")` (`from mcp.server.mcpserver.exceptions import ToolError`) for a failure
you expected, such as a bad argument. Its message reaches the model, which can correct the
call. Any other exception shows the caller only `Error executing tool <name>`, and its text
stays in this server's log, because an unexpected error can reveal internals.

